experiment: repdrift
  the question      §59c. Is the REP INDEX exchangeable? `seed + r * 7919` is
                    injective, so creature r is an independent draw and r must
                    carry NO information. §59b's archived matrix says otherwise
                    for ONE arm: T1.RET.D drifts upward with r in 30 of its 36
                    windows, in e1 alone (t +2.77, where nothing is learned yet),
                    while T1.RET / T1.ORACLE.D / T1.ORACLE read 5 / 1 / 0 on the
                    same windows. Imbalance is refuted as the cause (survives at
                    2.53 SE) and so is a weak coin (xoshiro256++ via splitmix64).
  the primary       the OLS slope of T1.RET.D's e1-e8 slot term on r, over
                    r 96..287 -- creatures NO section has scored. Null: slope = 0.
  what it cannot    the four-block trend DECELERATES, so the drift may have
   do               SATURATED before r 96. REFUTED here means `no slope in this
                    range`, NOT `the r 0..95 drift was noise`. Secondary 2 is
                    what makes saturation visible rather than assumed.
  session           900.0 s of simulated life x 324 sessions
  range             fresh r 96..287   G0 on (r 0..35)   arm2 on

  THE VERDICT'S FOUR BRANCHES, exercised before any real row reaches it
  (`deferral-note-is-untested-code`: real rows cannot test a branch they
  never take, and a CONSTANT REGRESSOR cannot fail a slope test at all).
    slope +0.00300  se 0.000288  sxx 5.0e+05 -> CONFIRMED -- the rep index carries information   ok  (slope >= +2 SE)
    slope +0.00020  se 0.000288  sxx 5.0e+05 -> REFUTED -- no slope in this range                ok  (|slope| < 2 SE)
    slope -0.00300  se 0.000288  sxx 5.0e+05 -> REVERSED -- the drift's sign is not stable       ok  (slope <= -2 SE)
    slope +0.00300  se 0.000288  sxx 0.0e+00 -> UNDEFINED -- unidentified or too few creatures   ok  (CONSTANT regressor: sxx = 0)
    branches reachable  4/4  PASS

  THE PER-CREATURE DUMP. `%a` is the exact bit pattern, so two runs over
  DIFFERENT rep ranges can be compared with `diff` and no tolerance. GATE A
  is that comparison: independent creatures must agree to the last bit, and
  a single differing creature is a harness defect touching every experiment.
